{
  "id": "COMPOUND:1081 - DISEASE:2183",
  "verbalization": "Capecitabine, a chemotherapy agent, appears to influence multiple biological pathways that ultimately contribute to the development of Neck Pain. The evidence suggests that capecitabine affects macrophage stimulating 1, which in turn causes Haemangiopericytoma and Neck Pain. Similarly, capecitabine influences CF transmembrane conductance regulator, leading to Aspergillosis and Neck Pain. Additionally, capecitabine interacts with EF-hand domain containing 1, which is linked to Natal tooth and then to CF transmembrane conductance regulator, again resulting in Neck Pain. These pathways collectively highlight a potential mechanism by which capecitabine may contribute to the development of Neck Pain through indirect, multi-step interactions involving immune and cellular signaling processes. The integration of these pathways underscores the complexity of the relationship, emphasizing the role of macrophage activity and cellular signaling in the pathophysiology of Neck Pain.",
